    "lawCode": "Amd §24-0301, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5642\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to elec-\ntronic filing of freshwater wetlands regulatory maps\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe  purpose  of this bill is to improve the availability of information\non the location of regulated freshwater wetlands.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nECL §24-0301 is amended to authorize the Commissioner of the  Department\nof  Environmental  Conservation (DEC) to file electronic versions of the\nfreshwater wetlands regulatory maps with  interested  local  governments\nand  to  authorize  posting  of  the  official  maps on the Department's\nwebsite.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPursuant to the Freshwater Wetland Act, the Commissioner of the  Depart-\nment  is  required  to  file copies of draft maps with local governments\nwhere they can be made \"available for public inspection at a  convenient\nlocation  in  such  local  government.\"  Subsequent to public review and\ncomment, the Commissioner then finalizes the maps and files a final copy\nof the map with the local government. These maps assist  the  public  in\ndetermining  where  protected  wetlands  occur.  In  addition, the local\ngovernments use the maps for local planning and decision making. Through\nhistoric practice, the expectation is that the maps must be produced and\ndisseminated as paper maps to be \"official.\" This precludes the  use  of\ncurrent  technological  advances to provide the maps as digital informa-\ntion. This  legislation,  by  specifically  authorizing  the  electronic\nfiling of maps, offers the following benefits:\n\n(1) it legitimizes the electronic format of the maps, making them legal-\nly useable for decision making;\n\n(2)  it  allows for a less expensive means for the Department to provide\nmaps to local governments;\n\n(3) if a local government has the data as an  electronic  file,  it  can\nbetter  serve  the public and its own decision-making entities by making\ncopies of maps as needed; and\n\n(4) if a local government has digital files of maps,  it  can  post  the\nmaps on its own website.\n\nThe  posting  of  freshwater  wetlands  maps on the Department's website\nwould provide the public with much  better  access  to  the  maps  under\n\nconditions  more  convenient  to  them  and  it  would save considerable\nDepartment and local government staff  time.  For  example,  most  local\ngovernment  clerks'  offices are not open in the evening or on weekends,\nwhen  landowners may be interested in checking on the status of property\nthey are considering buying. If the maps  were  available  on  the  web,\nlandowners would be able to check the maps from the convenience of their\nhomes  at  any  time  of day. Furthermore, it would significantly reduce\ninquiries to regional offices  and  to  local  government  clerks  about\naccess to maps or whether particular properties are considered wetlands.\nAlthough these are seemingly simple questions, they consume considerable\nstaff time. Having access to maps on the web will also enable members of\nthe  public to determine whether an activity they see happening in their\narea (e.g., clearing near a \"wet area\") is occurring in the vicinity  of\na  protected  wetland.  This will allay -- or confirm -- their concerns,\nand will reduce the  number  of  violations  reported  to  the  regional\noffices  for actions over which the Department has no jurisdiction. With\nso many other important information sources available on  the  web,  the\nexpectation  is  that  the regulatory maps should be there as well. This\namendment will greatly enhance the utility of the web-posted maps,  once\nthey are posted, by making the \"official\" versions of the map available.\nPosting  freshwater wetlands maps on the web requires technical capacity\nthat is not currently available to the Department. However, this  legis-\nlation,  by  specifically authorizing the Department to post the maps on\nthe website, while not mandating such posting, will allow the Department\nto post maps on its website at such time as the Department has the abil-\nity to do so. Further, it would also give maps posted on the website the\nofficial status as regulatory documents.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA.11463 of 2004; passed Assembly\nA.3949 of 2005; passed Assembly\nA.3949 of 2006; passed Assembly/S.7095 of 2006; environmental  conserva-\ntion\nA.2691 of 2007 passed Assembly/S.2840 of 2008 environmental conservation\nA.2691 of 2008 passed Assembly/S.2840 of 2008 environmental conservation\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nElectronically  filing  maps in lieu of producing and distributing paper\nmaps ultimately could result in cost savings to the  State.  Because  of\nlimited  number  of map amendments ongoing in the State, and the limited\nnumber of local governments currently capable of effectively  using  the\ndigital  data  and  therefore  requiring paper maps, the savings are not\nlikely to be substantial in the short term. It  would  take  an  initial\ninvestment  of  resources  to  get maps posted on the web; however, once\nposted, their availability would save regional staff  considerable  time\nin responding to public inquiries for the map information while increas-\ning landowner convenience.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nOn the thirtieth day after it shall have become a law.\n\n",
